Most of us learned that every fourth year is a leap year.
    draw h1900 h2000

Yet 1900 (@draw s1900 n1900@) had no twenty-ninth of February, (@draw n2000@) and 2000 did, so what is the rule really for?

    write id w_year text "**Solar year** ≈ 365 days 5 h 49 min"

It starts with the sun: from one spring to the next takes 365 days, plus almost six (@draw f1 l_f1 ends@) hours.
    draw day_box lbl_day q_ticks

A calendar of exactly 365 days drops those (@draw f2@) hours, so each year spring arrives about six hours later by the date.

After four years the leftovers come to nearly a whole day, and so Julius Caesar's calendar added (@draw l_feb@) one: a leap day every fourth year.
    draw f3 f4

    write id w_julian text "**Julian rule**: every 4th year is a leap year"

But four lots of five hours forty-nine make twenty-three hours sixteen, not twenty-four. (@draw gap_arrow gap_lbl ends@)

So every leap day adds about three quarters of an hour too much, which is eleven minutes for every year.
    write id w_over text "Leap day: about **11 min a year** too much"

    erase daybar

Eleven minutes is nothing in a lifetime, but by 1582 the calendar had run ten days ahead (@draw zero l_zero@) of where the Church had fixed (@draw up l_up ends@) the spring equinox in 325, the day Easter is counted from: it now fell on (@write text "Equinox: 21 → 11 March" temp true@) the eleventh of March.
    draw ax years_axis

So Pope Gregory (@erase l_up@) the Thirteenth (@erase up@) (@draw cut l_cut@) cut the ten days out: Thursday the fourth (@write id w_1582 text "**1582**: 10 days cut — Thu 4 Oct → Fri 15 Oct"@) of October 1582 was followed by Friday the fifteenth.

That reset the calendar, (@draw jul@) but with the old rule it would simply drift away again: four more days by (@draw l_jul pj l_pj ends@) 2100.

    erase cut l_cut

The fix is to drop a leap day now and then.
    highlight jul

    write id w_cent text "**Except** century years: 1700, 1800, 1900"

A century of overshoots comes to about three quarters of a day, so the century (@draw g1700 ends@) years lose their leap (@draw g1800 ends@) day: 1700, 1800 and (@draw g1900 ends@) 1900.

But three quarters is not a whole day, so skipping every (@point at.data [1900,-0.52] gesture circle@) century would cut one day too many in four hundred years.

    write id w_400 text "**Except** every 400th year: 2000, 2400"

And so the centuries divisible by four hundred (@draw greg2 l_greg p2000 l_2000@) keep their leap day, which is why 2000 had its twenty-ninth of February.

That makes 97 leap days in every four hundred years, and the error falls from eleven minutes a year to under half a minute.
    write id w_97 text "100 − 3 = **97** leap days in 400 years"

Not everyone made the switch: some Orthodox churches still keep the Julian calendar, so for them 1900 was a leap year.
    highlight jul

    write text "10 + 3 = **13 days**" temp true

So their gap grew to thirteen (@draw gap3 l_gap3@) days, and their Christmas, the twenty-fifth of December, now falls on our seventh of January.

A leap year is a correction, 1900 was a correction of the correction, and 2000 corrected that one in turn.
    highlight w_julian w_cent w_400 effect box

    erase gap3 l_gap3 l_pj l_2000

    hide ax years_axis zero l_zero jul l_jul pj g1700 g1800 g1900 greg2 l_greg p2000

    cards years bins ["Leap year","Not a leap year"] check each
        * "2024" bin "Leap year"
        * "2100" bin "Not a leap year"
        * "2400" bin "Leap year"
        * "1600" bin "Leap year"
        * "1800" bin "Not a leap year"

    ask "Leap year or not? Drag each year into its box." intro "Your turn." on years stage own store f
        right: {f} of {f.total} right.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try.

    erase years

    show ax years_axis zero l_zero jul l_jul pj g1700 g1800 g1900 greg2 l_greg p2000

The next February without a twenty-ninth is in 2100, and now you know why.
    point at.data [2100,1.04] gesture circle

    pause 1

